<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5171">
<a:ObjectID>6C721690-06E3-4F34-8D3D-4CEE98CD9258</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>POINT</a:Name>
<a:Code>POINT</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>0-dimensional geometric primitive, representing a position.</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5178">
<a:ObjectID>22BB0DA2-D1D4-4EF1-B3EF-38C8BD47605A</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>POS</a:Name>
<a:Code>POS</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>One of a sequence of numbers designating the position of a point in N-dimensional space</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5175">
<a:ObjectID>840E1803-87F0-4B23-9000-824D36C13A9B</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>SURFACE</a:Name>
<a:Code>SURFACE</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A Surface is a 2-dimensional primitive and is composed of one or more surface patches. The surface patches are connected to one another.
The orientation of the surface is positive (&quot;up&quot;). The orientation of a surface chooses an &quot;up&quot; direction through the choice of the upward normal, which, if the surface is not a cycle, is the side of the surface from which the exterior boundary appears counterclockwise. Reversal of the surface orientation reverses the curve orientation of each boundary component, and interchanges the conceptual &quot;up&quot; and &quot;down&quot; direction of the surface. If the surface is the boundary of a solid, the &quot;up&quot; direction is usually outward. For closed surfaces, which have no boundary, the up direction is that of the surface patches, which must be consistent with one another. Its included surface patches describe the interior structure of the Surface
</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5177">
<a:ObjectID>9ED4524C-3DBC-4A68-8127-941137248D51</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>COORDINATEREFERENCESYSTEM</a:Name>
<a:Code>COORDINATEREFERENCESYSTEM</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Association to a coordinate reference system, either referencing or containing the definition of that reference system. </a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5174">
<a:ObjectID>013D402E-0B99-4FE0-9475-7120564FF43C</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>CURVE</a:Name>
<a:Code>CURVE</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>1-dimensional geometric primitive, representing the continuous image of a line.  

NOTE The boundary of a curve is the set of points at either end of the curve. If the curve is a cycle, the two ends are identical, and the curve (if topologically closed) is considered to not have a boundary. The first point is called the start point, and the last is the end point. Connectivity of the curve is guaranteed by the &quot;continuous image of a line&quot; clause. A topological theorem states that a continuous image of a connected
set is connected.
</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5182">
<a:ObjectID>DAFBA375-8114-4A41-B190-7FF3134B07FC</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>ENVELOPE</a:Name>
<a:Code>ENVELOPE</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Envelope defines an extent using a pair of positions defining opposite corners in arbitrary dimensions. The first direct position is the &quot;lower corner&quot; (a coordinate position consisting of all the minimal ordinates for each dimension for all points within the envelope), the second one the &quot;upper corner&quot; (a coordinate position consisting of all the maximal ordinates for each dimension for all points within the envelope).
</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
<c:Attributes>
<o:Attribute Id="o5310">
<a:ObjectID>AA2DCED7-DD83-4190-B3FD-FF860BD0A242</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>LowerCorner</a:Name>
<a:Code>LowerCorner</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A coordinate position consisting of all the minimal ordinates for each dimension for all points within the envelope.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>int</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
<o:Attribute Id="o5311">
<a:ObjectID>FFCE1F1C-A759-49A3-9252-19CE288CD24D</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>UpperCorner</a:Name>
<a:Code>UpperCorner</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A coordinate position consisting of all the maximal ordinates for each dimension for all points within the envelope.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>int</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
<o:Attribute Id="o5312">
<a:ObjectID>36FD1EF5-5AB7-450E-9050-758C4023E1CE</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>POS</a:Name>
<a:Code>POS</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A number designating the position of a point in N-dimensional space</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>int</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
</c:Attributes>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5183">
<a:ObjectID>1B7F8FA7-F13F-4E71-BBEE-C66B3EDAD8B7</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>FEATURE</a:Name>
<a:Code>FEATURE</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A geographic feature is an abstraction of a real world phenomenon; it is a geographic feature if it is associated with a location relative to the Earth or an abstract reference system.  So a digital representation of the real world can be thought of as a set of features. The state of a feature is defined by a set of properties, where each property can be thought of as a {name, type, value} triple.  </a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5181">
<a:ObjectID>C60D0EE5-DB68-4C1C-8EA3-A5BA8A398C82</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>FEATURECOLLECTION</a:Name>
<a:Code>FEATURECOLLECTION</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>A GML Feature Collection is a collection of GML feature instances that can behave as a GML feature.</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5176">
<a:ObjectID>E58C4E64-6F6E-424C-93B9-6C4761BEEA93</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>GEOMETRY</a:Name>
<a:Code>GEOMETRY</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Class of object that describes the location, shape or extent of a geographic feature.</a:Comment>
<a:Stereotype>GML</a:Stereotype>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5215">
<a:ObjectID>1FAAAB42-F0C5-4E03-B3CE-03D356EA055C</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>GmlDiagramObject</a:Name>
<a:Code>GmlDiagramObject</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Any of the magnitudes that serve to define the position of a point by reference to a fixed figure, system of lines, etc.</a:Comment>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
</o:Class>
<o:Class Id="o5220">
<a:ObjectID>48A12AB4-2BAC-4014-A2BA-42D9423F5429</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>GmlPosition</a:Name>
<a:Code>GmlPosition</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>Position described by a single set of coordinates within a coordinate reference system.

Note that a sequence of x/y coordinate pairs may be specified to provide the physical location of non-point oriented objects like Cables, Ducts, etc. or for describing an area of an object like a substation or georgraphical zone.  

</a:Comment>
<a:UseParentNamespace>0</a:UseParentNamespace>
<a:Persistence>T</a:Persistence>
<a:Cardinality>*</a:Cardinality>
<c:Attributes>
<o:Attribute Id="o5313">
<a:ObjectID>CD661299-222F-4158-B622-0F87C18741C9</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>xPosition</a:Name>
<a:Code>xPosition</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>The position along the X axis of  the coordinate reference system.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>LongDouble</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
<o:Attribute Id="o5314">
<a:ObjectID>1C34F86E-363C-4E7B-8E15-A88B7627C5AE</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>yPosition</a:Name>
<a:Code>yPosition</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>The position along the Y axis of  the coordinate reference system.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>LongDouble</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
<o:Attribute Id="o5315">
<a:ObjectID>1331E49F-CDC6-4AAB-96AA-6E490082FF71</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>sequenceNumber</a:Name>
<a:Code>sequenceNumber</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>The position of this position within a series of positions.  It is zero relative.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>Integer</a:DataType>
<a:InitialValue>0</a:InitialValue>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
<o:Attribute Id="o5316">
<a:ObjectID>25AC9324-F14E-48FD-B07E-60763D2E1A62</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>zPosition</a:Name>
<a:Code>zPosition</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1202478865</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>sechin</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1202478865</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>sechin</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>If applicable,the position along the Z axis of  the coordinate reference system.</a:Comment>
<a:DataType>LongDouble</a:DataType>
<a:Persistent>0</a:Persistent>
</o:Attribute>
</c:Attributes>
</o:Class>
